{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful noble who served as the Count of Tripoli during the tumultuous period of the Crusades in the Holy Land. He held this title from 1152 until his death in 1187 and played a central role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His family lineage connected him to many important figures in the region and gave him significant influence over military campaigns and diplomatic negotiations. Raymond spent much of his early life dealing with internal conflicts within the Crusader states while also facing threats from Muslim powers outside the borders.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born into a prominent family that had established strong ties with the Latin East. His father became involved in a struggle for control of Tripoli after the death of the previous ruler. Raymond eventually succeeded his father but faced immediate challenges from local rivals who sought to undermine his authority. He spent years consolidating his power and securing the borders of his county against external invasions. His ability to navigate these complex political waters earned him respect among his peers despite the constant danger surrounding him.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe death of King Baldwin IV created a power vacuum that Raymond filled as regent for the young heir. This position placed him at the center of fierce factional disputes within the kingdom. He argued for caution in military matters while his rivals pushed for aggressive action against Saladin. Raymond believed that the Crusader states needed to maintain unity and avoid rash decisions that could lead to disaster. His cautious approach often put him at odds with more aggressive nobles who sought glory through battle. These disagreements led to periods of intense civil strife that weakened the kingdom just before its greatest crisis.\n\n*Battle of Hattin and Death*\nThe final years of Raymond's life were marked by the catastrophic defeat at the Battle of Hattin where the Crusader armies were destroyed. Raymond managed to escape the slaughter but died shortly after from illness or perhaps wounds sustained during the conflict. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and removed a key stabilizing force from the region. The loss of his leadership contributed to the rapid collapse of the Crusader states in the following years. Historians view his career as a mix of political skill and tragic timing that ultimately could not prevent the fall of Jerusalem. His legacy remains a subject of debate regarding whether his cautious strategy could have saved the kingdom if fully supported by other leaders. The story of Raymond III illustrates the difficult choices faced by Christian rulers in the Levant during the twelfth century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.7418175e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0019453003}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.095287204}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.010270346}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -0.816988}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.010663659}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -1.1490877}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -3.6522722}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -1.6570228e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.023329211}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.0019658445}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.07711486}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.055866577}, {"token": " tumult", "logprob": -5.9068384}, {"token": "uous", "logprob": -1.4305125e-06}, {"token": " period", "logprob": -1.0452108}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00023528721}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -5.1260126e-06}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.011477883}, {"token": "ades", "logprob": -0.34324205}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.786643}, 